ABSTRACT

This paper presents a rigorously grounded theoretical framework for a Photonically‑Coupled Magnetostructural Skeletal Architecture (PCMSA) – a field‑organised embodiment paradigm for ultra‑light humanoid robotic modules. Departing from conventional tendon‑driven transmissions and rigid mechanical joints, the PCMSA achieves structural integrity, articulation, and distributed sensing through coordinated electromagnetic interactions embedded within hollow composite lattice structures, integrated Halbach magnetic couplings, embedded micro‑coil actuators, and differential polymer optical waveguide networks. The body is formalised as a five‑tuple over a constrained admissible state space; quasi‑static well‑posedness is proved. Under standard linearised dissipative magnetoelastic assumptions the coupled system admits exponential stability, and a constructive damping coefficient is derived. A novel differential photonic routing guarantees full structural observability with a diagonal sensing Jacobian, supported by a provable stiffness‑separation condition. Analytical models for holding force (310 N at contact, 185 N at 1 mm gap), actuator force‑to‑mass ratio (complete system, 80–180 N kg⁻¹), photonic strain resolution (0.12 dB deg⁻¹), and thermal equilibrium are presented, incorporating realistic material parameters, orthotropic composite shell buckling with NASA knockdown factors, Halbach field‑enhancement curves, and stroke‑to‑joint kinematic conversion. The actuator torque density is benchmarked against state‑of‑the‑art actuators, and the current limits are transparently scoped. A dedicated “Scope and Limitations” section acknowledges the architectural assumptions. A validation framework based on multiphysics simulation is specified, establishing the scientific defensibility of a modular field‑organised robot. The work is confined to physical embodiment; AI cognition and high‑level control are explicitly excluded.

Keywords: magnetostructural robotics, avian‑inspired lattice, Halbach coupling, distributed electromagnetic actuation, polymer optical waveguide sensing, field‑organised morphology, Lyapunov stability, structural observability, composite buckling.

