1. INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION

Contemporary electric humanoid robots share a common architectural paradigm: rotary motors, harmonic drives, tendon/cable routing, and rigid bolted assemblies. This leads to a Mass Escalation Inequality: total body mass m_{\text{total}} = m_a + m_s \ge m_a(1+k), where m_a is actuator mass, m_s is the structural mass needed to support the actuators, and k is a structural compliance constant. Because m_a itself is chosen to bear the total load, the relation becomes recursive and diverges unless k<1 and initial mass is extremely small. In practice, geared collaborative robots trade safety for speed: industrial arms reach end‑effector speeds of 11\,\text{m s}^{-1} while collaborative counterparts operate below 1\,\text{m s}^{-1} in full collaborative mode, a direct consequence of the mass–compliance trade‑off.

We propose a fundamentally different design: the Field‑Organised Robotic Body. Structural coherence, articulation, and sensing arise not from rigid mechanical fixation but from controlled physical fields acting continuously across the body. Inspiration is drawn from: (i) avian skeletal biomechanics – many bird bones are hollow (pneumatic) with internal struts angled at 25^\circ–60^\circ that resist torsional and bending loads while minimising weight; (ii) topology‑optimised lattice engineering – stress‑constrained topology optimisation of cellular structures can produce parts with extreme specific stiffness; (iii) soft photonic sensing – polymer optical waveguides have demonstrated multi‑modal force and deformation sensing immune to electromagnetic interference.

Related Work and Differentiation.
Recent literature has applied Halbach arrays to humanoid robot actuators. Wang et al. (2025) introduced a 3‑axis Halbach permanent magnet elastomer sensor that cancels crosstalk between adjacent sensing elements. A 2026 survey catalogues Halbach‑enhanced permanent magnet synchronous motors that improve torque density. A discretized axial‑flux Halbach array was used in a robotic end‑effector (IEEE/ASME TMECH, 2026). Tang et al. (2024) introduced a 3D‑printed differential optical waveguide design for soft robotic proprioception, demonstrating the power of differential intensity readout. However, none of these works combine passive Halbach structural coupling, distributed Lorentz actuation, and differential photonic observability within a single rigid‑lattice humanoid embodiment. To the author’s knowledge, no prior work unifies passive Halbach structural coupling, distributed Lorentz actuation, and differential photonic observability into a single humanoid embodiment framework. This distinction – field‑organised structure rather than field‑enhanced motor – is fundamental and unifies coupling, actuation, and sensing within a single magnetic architecture.

Contributions. This work (i) formally defines the PCMSA body as a five‑tuple and its admissible state space; (ii) presents a structural optimisation including orthotropic Brazier shell buckling with NASA knockdown factor (acknowledging its metallic‑shell origin), and a modal analysis; (iii) develops a Halbach‑enhanced magnetic coupling model with force‑vs‑gap curves and inter‑module crosstalk analysis; (iv) provides a joint‑level force‑to‑mass and torque‑to‑mass analysis with stroke‑to‑angular‑range conversion, transparently scoping the actuator’s torque‑density limitations; (v) proves EMI‑immune photonic sensing with differential waveguide pairs, citing recent soft‑robotic proprioception work; (vi) proves linearised exponential stability via a corrected Lyapunov functional with constructive damping coefficient; (vii) proves full structural observability under a provable stiffness‑separation condition; (viii) outlines a multiphysics validation framework, a power‑budget scalability study, and a dedicated Scope and Limitations section.

