3. STRUCTURAL ARCHITECTURE: HOLLOW AVIAN‑INSPIRED LATTICE MODULE

3.1 Geometric Model

Each module M_i is a hollow cylinder of length L_i, outer radius R_{\text{outer}}, inner radius R_{\text{inner}}. The hollowness ratio is \eta = (R_{\text{inner}}/R_{\text{outer}})^2. An internal lattice of N_{\text{strut}} slender CFRP rods crosses the core at angles \theta_k\in[25^\circ,60^\circ] to the bone axis. The outer shell thickness is t_c = R_{\text{outer}}-R_{\text{inner}}.

Layup: The tube wall is a symmetric [0^\circ/90^\circ]_{\text{s}} cross‑ply laminate of unidirectional CFRP plies, with fibre volume fraction V_f=0.6. Effective engineering constants are derived via classical lamination theory. Homogenised orthotropic properties: E_L = 135\,\text{GPa} (axial), E_T = 10\,\text{GPa} (hoop), G_{LT}=5\,\text{GPa}, \nu_{LT}=0.25. Density \rho_c = 1570\,\text{kg m}^{-3}. Failure is evaluated via the Tsai–Wu criterion with X_T=2550\,\text{MPa}, X_C=1500\,\text{MPa}.

3.2 Mass Model

m_i = m_{\text{shell}} + m_{\text{struts}} + m_{\text{Halbach rings}} + m_{\text{coils}} + m_{\text{wg}}

with

\begin{aligned}
m_{\text{shell}} &= \rho_c \pi (R_{\text{outer}}^2-R_{\text{inner}}^2)L_i,\\
m_{\text{struts}} &= N_{\text{strut}}\rho_c A_{\text{strut}} (2R_{\text{inner}}/\sin\theta_k),\\
m_{\text{Halbach rings}} &= 2 \times \rho_{\text{mag}} \pi (R_m^2 - R_{m,\text{in}}^2) h_m,\quad \rho_{\text{mag}}=7500\,\text{kg m}^{-3}.
\end{aligned}

3.3 Bending, Orthotropic Brazier Buckling, and Optimal Hollowness

Under a tip load F_{\text{tip}} the maximum bending stress occurs at the outer fibre. The critical buckling moment must account for the Brazier effect in laminated composite cylinders. The classical isotropic formula is not directly applicable. For a [0^\circ/90^\circ]_{\text{s}} cross‑ply tube we adopt a semi‑empirical orthotropic shell buckling expression based on the nonlinear Brazier analysis of composite cylinders (Tatting et al., 1996, AIAA J.). The NASA SP‑8007 knockdown factor \kappa is widely used for imperfection‑sensitive shells, although it was originally developed for isotropic metallic cylinders. Recent NASA studies have employed a universal knockdown factor of 0.65 for composite shell designs, while noting that the technical justification is not fully established. We adopt \kappa = 0.65 as a conservative engineering margin, acknowledging this limitation. The critical bending moment becomes

M_{\text{cr}} = \kappa \, \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{9} \frac{\pi E_{\text{hoop}} t_c^2 R_{\text{outer}}}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL})}},

where E_{\text{hoop}} \approx \frac{1}{2}(E_T + E_L) \approx 72.5\,\text{GPa}. The minimum wall thickness to avoid buckling under a design moment M = F_{\text{tip}} L_i is

t_{\text{Brazier}} = \left( \frac{9\sqrt{3(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL})} M}{2\sqrt{2}\,\kappa\,\pi E_{\text{hoop}} R_{\text{outer}}} \right)^{1/2}.

Theorem 3.1 (Optimal Hollowness with Composite Buckling).
The mass‑optimal hollowness ratio \eta^* for a bending‑critical module subject to stress and composite Brazier buckling constraints is

\eta^* = \max\Big\{0,\; 1 - \frac{2 t_{\min}}{D_{\text{outer}}}\Big\},
\qquad
t_{\min} = \max\!\big(t_{\text{stress}},\; t_{\text{Brazier}}\big),

with t_{\text{stress}} derived from bending stress: \sigma_{\max} = \frac{32 F_{\text{tip}} L_i}{\pi D_{\text{outer}}^3(1-\eta^2)} \le \sigma_{\text{yield}}.

Numerical example: D_{\text{outer}}=30\,\text{mm}, L_i=200\,\text{mm}, F_{\text{tip}}=50\,\text{N}. Using the orthotropic Brazier formula with \kappa=0.65 gives t_{\text{Brazier}}\approx 0.62\,\text{mm}, dominating. Hence \eta^* \approx 0.917 (inner radius \approx 13.8\,\text{mm}). This realistic value is used throughout.

3.4 Strut Stability

Theorem 3.2 (Strut Buckling).
Each internal strut of radius r_s and length L_{\text{strut}} = 2R_{\text{inner}}/\sin\theta_k resists local buckling provided

N_{\text{strut}} \ge \frac{P_{\text{distributed}} \, 4 L_{\text{strut}}^2}{\pi^3 E_L r_s^4}.

3.5 Dynamic Structural Response (Modal Analysis)

The first bending natural frequency of a cantilever module is

\omega_1 = \frac{(1.875)^2}{L_i^2}\sqrt{\frac{E_L I}{\rho_c A}} ,

where I = \frac{\pi}{4}(R_{\text{outer}}^4 - R_{\text{inner}}^4) and A = \pi(R_{\text{outer}}^2-R_{\text{inner}}^2). For the optimised phalanx module (L_i=60\,\text{mm}, R_{\text{outer}}=9\,\text{mm}, \eta=0.85), f_1 = \omega_1/2\pi \approx 480\,\text{Hz}, far above locomotion and actuation control bandwidths, justifying quasi‑static structural assumptions and simplifying the observability analysis (Section 8).

