4. MAGNETIC COUPLING VIA HALBACH ARRAYS

4.1 Halbach Ring Geometry and Field Enhancement

Each inter‑module coupling uses two identical coaxial Halbach rings, each with N_{\text{seg}}\ge 8 magnet segments (B_r = 1.45\,\text{T}, N52SH grade, thickness h_m). The flux density on the axis at distance z from the face is expressed via a Fourier expansion. The peak gap field is

B_{\text{gap}}(g) = B_r \, \kappa_H(g) \, f_{\text{fill}},

where f_{\text{fill}} \approx 0.95 accounts for segment gaps, and the enhancement factor \kappa_H(g) is, using the fundamental harmonic,

\kappa_H(g) = \frac{4}{\pi} \sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{N_{\text{seg}}}\right) \frac{e^{-g/R_m}}{1 - e^{-2h_m/R_m}} \cdot \cosh\!\left(\frac{g}{2R_m}\right),

R_m = (R_{\text{outer}}+R_{\text{inner}})/2. For N_{\text{seg}}=12, h_m=3\,\text{mm}, R_m=8\,\text{mm}: \kappa_H(0) \approx 1.42, \kappa_H(1\,\text{mm}) \approx 1.21. A full curve is shown in Fig. 1.

4.2 Holding Force and Angular Stiffness

The axial holding force, including a first‑order demagnetisation correction, is

F_{\text{hold}}(g) = \frac{B_{\text{gap}}(g)^2 A_{\text{eff}}}{2\mu_0} \, C_{\text{gap}}(g),\qquad
C_{\text{gap}}(g) \approx 1 - \frac{\mu_r-1}{\mu_r}\frac{g}{g+h_m},

\mu_r\approx 1.05. For the ring dimensions (R_m=10\,\text{mm}, R_{m,\text{in}}=6\,\text{mm}, h_m=3\,\text{mm}):

· At contact (g=0): F_{\text{hold}} \approx 310\,\text{N}.
· At working gap g=1\,\text{mm}: F_{\text{hold}} \approx 185\,\text{N}.

Angular misalignment \phi generates a restoring torque T_{\text{restore}} \approx -K_\phi \phi with K_\phi \approx \frac{F_{\text{hold}}(g) R_{\text{eff}}}{2}, R_{\text{eff}} \approx R_m. At g=1\,\text{mm}, K_\phi \approx 1.5\,\text{N·m rad}^{-1}. The interface unlocks at a critical angle, acting as a mechanical overload protection.

4.3 Inter‑Module Magnetic Crosstalk Analysis

In multi‑joint assemblies the stray field at a distance d from a Halbach ring can be approximated by a dipole moment m = B_r V_{\text{mag}}/\mu_0. The axial stray field is B_{\text{stray}} \approx \frac{\mu_0}{2\pi}\frac{m}{d^3}. For the ring volume V_{\text{mag}} \approx 1.5\,\text{cm}^3 and a finger‑phalanx spacing d=40\,\text{mm}, B_{\text{stray}} \approx 0.8\,\text{mT}, less than 1% of the gap field, causing force perturbation below 2%. This is well within the 3.9% crosstalk threshold reported for independent magnetic robot control (Davy et al., 2023). For closer spacings (d < 30\,\text{mm}), a 0.1 mm mu‑metal foil liner reduces stray field by a factor >10.

