5. DISTRIBUTED ELECTROMAGNETIC ACTUATION AND JOINT KINEMATICS

5.1 Lorentz Force and Coil Design

Each actuator is a rectangular micro‑coil of N turns, dimensions w \times l, immersed in the local field B_0 = B_{\text{gap}}(g_{\text{act}}). For a square coil (w=l), the Lorentz force is

F_L = 2 N I w B_0 .

Coil alone gives a high ideal force‑to‑mass ratio; the complete actuator includes the Halbach source. Using a magnet of mass m_{\text{mag}} \approx 11.3\,\text{g} to generate B_0 = 0.8\,\text{T} over a 2 mm gap, and a coil of mass m_{\text{coil}} \approx 2\,\text{g}, total mass m_{\text{act}} \approx 15\,\text{g}. At thermally limited current I=1\,\text{A}, F_L = 0.8\,\text{N}, giving a system force‑to‑mass ratio

\Gamma_{\text{sys}} \approx \frac{0.8\,\text{N}}{0.015\,\text{kg}} \approx 53\,\text{N kg}^{-1}.

A refined design pushes this to 80–180\,\text{N kg}^{-1}.

5.2 Stroke‑to‑Joint‑Angle Kinematics and Torque

Lorentz actuators have limited stroke s_{\max}. The achievable joint angle range per actuator module is \Delta q = s_{\max} / r, where r is the effective moment arm. With s_{\max}=5\,\text{mm} and r=15\,\text{mm}, \Delta q \approx 0.33\,\text{rad} \approx 19^\circ. A distributed actuator ring with two opposed coils gives a range of about 38^\circ. Peak torque is T_{\text{peak}} = F_L r = 0.8 \times 0.015 = 12\,\text{mN·m}.

Torque Density and Scope.
Table 1 benchmarks the PCMSA actuator against state‑of‑the‑art technologies. The current single‑coil torque density is two orders of magnitude below quasi‑direct‑drive and MR‑clutch actuators. Consequently, the PCMSA actuator is proposed as a fine‑motion, structurally integrated distributed actuation substrate, suited for dexterous low‑load articulation, micro‑adjustment stabilisation, compliant grasp modulation, and distributed morphological shaping, rather than for macro‑load‑bearing ballistic limb actuation. High‑torque primary joints may still require supplementary actuators; the PCMSA’s value lies in eliminating mechanical transmissions for the many smaller‑scale degrees of freedom that define human‑like dexterity.

