5.3 Thermal Constraint and Active Damping

Joule power P_J = I^2 R_{\text{coil}} is balanced by convection. The maximum continuous current is set by T_{\text{coil}} \le 80^\circ\text{C} (N52) or 150^\circ\text{C} (SH). When the joint moves, back‑EMF \mathcal{E} = -N w B_0 \dot{q} r induces a current if the coil is connected to a resistive load R_d. The resulting damping force is

F_d = - \frac{(N w B_0)^2}{R_d}\, \dot{u} = -c_{\text{em}} \dot{u}.

An H‑bridge switches between actuation (current source) and damping (resistive load) with sub‑millisecond transition times (schematic in Fig. 2). This damping term is used in the stability proof.

