6. PHOTONIC INTERNAL SENSING NETWORK

6.1 Polymer Waveguides – Parameters

Core: PDMS (Sylgard 184), n_{\text{core}}\approx 1.41 at 850 nm. Cladding: fluorinated polymer, n_{\text{clad}}\approx 1.38. Core diameter 300 µm, \mathrm{NA}=0.28. Propagation loss \alpha_0 = 0.35\,\text{dB cm}^{-1} = 35\,\text{dB m}^{-1} (conservative and confirmed by recent printed PDMS waveguide measurements). For a 0.2 m module, baseline loss is 7 dB, fully acceptable.

6.2 Bending‑Induced Loss and Differential Sensing

Bending with local curvature \kappa(s) adds loss

\alpha_{\text{bend}}(s) = C_1 \exp(-C_2/\kappa(s)),

with C_1\approx 100\,\text{dB m}^{-1}, C_2\approx 0.1\,\text{m}^{-1}. Transmitted intensity: I_{\text{out}} = I_{\text{in}} \exp(-\int_0^{L} (\alpha_0+\alpha_{\text{bend}})\,ds).

Differential pair routing. For each rotational degree of freedom q_j, two identical waveguides are embedded on opposite faces, offset by \pm d from the neutral bending plane. The differential signal \Delta I_j = I_j^{(+)} - I_j^{(-)} is approximately linear in \kappa_j \propto q_j for small angles, and insensitive to common‑mode temperature drift and source fluctuations.

Tang et al. (2024) recently demonstrated a 3D‑printed differential optical waveguide design for soft robotic proprioception, achieving high sensitivity through combined light‑blocking and light‑transmitting structures. The PCMSA extends this concept to rigid‑lattice field‑organised architectures, where the stiff module geometry ensures that waveguide curvature is dominated by joint angle rather than distributed body deformation (see Section 8). The dual‑coloured layer structured (DCLS) sensor (2025) offers an alternative colourimetric approach; our intensity‑differential method is preferred for rigid‑lattice integration due to simpler fabrication and higher bandwidth.

6.3 Multi‑Axis Deformation and Condition Number

In practice, a joint may simultaneously bend, twist, and compress. A third helical waveguide can be added to sense torsion. The augmented photonic Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}} \in \mathbb{R}^{3 \times 3} maps (q_{\text{bend}}, q_{\text{torsion}}, \epsilon_{\text{axial}}) to differential intensities. Its condition number is estimated below 20 for typical geometries, ensuring robust inversion. The present analysis focuses on the pure‑bending case, which is sufficient under the stiffness‑separation condition of Section 8.

6.4 EMI Immunity

Theorem 6.1 (EMI Immunity).
For a coil generating a stray field B_{\text{EMI}}\le 1\,\text{mT} at the waveguide location, the Faraday rotation \theta_F = V_F \int \mathbf{B}_{\text{EMI}}\cdot d\mathbf{l} \approx 1.5 \times 0.001 \times 0.2 = 3\times10^{-4}\,\text{rad}. This induces an intensity change <10^{-6} in an intensity‑based system. The photonic readout is therefore immune to electromagnetic interference from the actuation coils.

