7. COUPLED FIELD‑MECHANICAL STABILITY

7.1 Governing Equations

The displacement field \mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x},t), magnetic field \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x},t), and temperature T(\mathbf{x},t) satisfy

\begin{aligned}
\rho \ddot{\mathbf{u}} - \nabla\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}(\mathbf{u}) &= \mathbf{f}_{\text{body}} + \mathbf{J}\times\mathbf{B},\\
\nabla\cdot\mathbf{B} &= 0,\\
\rho c_p \dot{T} - \nabla\cdot(k\nabla T) &= \frac{|\mathbf{J}|^2}{\sigma_e},
\end{aligned}

with \mathbf{B} = \mu_0(\mathbf{H}+\mathbf{M}), \mathbf{M} fixed in the permanent magnets.

7.2 Quasi‑Static Well‑Posedness

Theorem 7.1 (Existence and Uniqueness).
Under uniform ellipticity of \mathbf{C} and L^2 body loads, there exists a unique \mathbf{u}^*\in H^1(\Omega) satisfying the weak magneto‑elastic equilibrium. Proof via Korn’s inequality and Lax–Milgram, treating \mathbf{J}\times\mathbf{B} as a given external field; full coupling is handled by a fixed‑point iteration that converges for small magnetic gaps.

7.3 Linearised Stability with Active Damping

Consider small perturbations about the equilibrium. Define the perturbed energy functional (quadratic form):

\mathcal{E} = \frac12 \int_\Omega \rho |\dot{\mathbf{u}}|^2 \,d\mathbf{x} + \frac12 \int_\Omega \boldsymbol{\epsilon} : \mathbf{C} : \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \,d\mathbf{x} + \frac12 \int_\Omega \rho c_p (T-T_{\text{ref}})^2 \,d\mathbf{x} + \mathcal{E}_{\text{mag}},

where \mathcal{E}_{\text{mag}} is the magnetic co‑energy appropriate for systems with fixed magnetisation. Under the assumptions of small motions and a dissipative circuit connection (active damping), the time derivative satisfies

\frac{d\mathcal{E}}{dt} = - \int_\Omega c_{\text{eddy}}|\dot{\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}|^2 \,d\mathbf{x} - \int_\Omega c_{\text{em}} |\dot{\mathbf{u}}|^2 \,d\mathbf{x} - \int_{\partial\Omega} h_{\text{conv}} (T-T_{\text{amb}})^2 \,dS \le - \lambda \mathcal{E},

with a constructive damping coefficient

\lambda = \min\!\left( \frac{2c_{\text{em}}}{\rho_{\max}}, \frac{2k}{c_p \rho L_c^2}, \frac{2c_{\text{eddy}}}{C_{\min}} \right) > 0,

where L_c is a characteristic conduction length and the Poincaré inequality bounds the thermal dissipation. Therefore, the equilibrium admits exponential stability under standard linearised dissipative magnetoelastic assumptions. For large motions with moving permanent magnets, polynomial stability is more typical; the linearised analysis presented here is sufficient for the intended small‑perturbation operational regime.

