8. OBSERVABILITY VIA DIFFERENTIAL PHOTONIC PAIRS

8.1 Stiffness‑Separation Condition

For the photonic Jacobian to remain diagonal, the module must be significantly stiffer than the joint so that waveguide bending between joints is negligible. Let k_j be the joint angular stiffness and k_m the module bending stiffness (cantilever). The stiffness ratio

R_{\text{stiff}} = \frac{k_m}{k_j L_i}

must exceed 20. For the phalanx module: k_m = 3E_L I / L_i^3 \approx 1.2\times 10^4\,\text{N m}^{-1}, k_j = K_\phi/L_i \approx 25\,\text{N m rad}^{-1}, giving R_{\text{stiff}} \approx 480 \gg 20. The condition is comfortably satisfied.

8.2 Observability Proof

Theorem 8.1 (Full Observability).
With the stiffness‑separation condition satisfied, the photonic Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}} \in \mathbb{R}^{2N_{\text{DOF}}\times N_{\text{DOF}}} mapping joint angles \mathbf{q} to differential intensities \boldsymbol{\Delta}\mathbf{I} is strictly diagonal with nonzero entries S_j. Hence \operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}) = N_{\text{DOF}} globally, and the structural state is locally observable from photonic measurements alone. The condition number \text{cond}(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}) = \max_j |S_j| / \min_j |S_j| remains below 10 for the design range, ensuring robust inversion.

