11. VALIDATION FRAMEWORK

The theory is validated through a tiered computational and experimental programme.

Multiphysics Simulation (Immediate). A single phalanx module (length 60 mm, D_{\text{outer}}=18\,\text{mm}, \eta=0.85, Halbach rings N52SH, two differential waveguide pairs) is modelled in a coupled FEM environment (COMSOL/Ansys Maxwell). Numerical validations:

· Magnetostatics: Holding force vs. gap and angular restoring torque; agreement with analytical predictions to within 15% after fringing correction.
· Thermal–electromagnetic: Coil temperature under continuous I=1\,\text{A}; steady‑state T<80^\circ\text{C} with natural convection.
· Photonics: Ray‑tracing/beam‑propagation to extract \Delta I vs. joint angle; confirm linear range and sensitivity \ge 0.1\,\text{dB deg}^{-1}.
· Coupled load test: 50 N tip load; verify no buckling, Tsai–Wu index < 0.5.

Performance Benchmarks. The module is expected to achieve: holding force \ge 185\,\text{N} at 1 mm gap; angular stiffness \ge 1.5\,\text{N·m rad}^{-1}; actuator force‑to‑mass \Gamma_{\text{sys}} \approx 80–180\,\text{N kg}^{-1}; photonic resolution <0.5^\circ over \pm 30^\circ; thermal safety margin >1.5; and a stability damping rate \lambda > 5\,\text{s}^{-1}.

Path to Physical Prototype. Following successful simulation, the module will be additively manufactured (continuous‑fibre CFRP with embedded magnets and waveguides). A three‑segment finger and a lightweight arm segment will validate scaling and distributed field coordination. These steps are deferred to a companion experimental paper.

