12. DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION

The PCMSA framework re‑imagines the humanoid body as a field‑organised continuum, replacing mechanical fasteners with Halbach magnetic couplings, tendons with embedded electromagnetic actuators, and electrical wiring with polymer photonic networks. This paper provides a mathematically rigorous foundation, including:

· Formal five‑tuple system definition and admissible state space;
· Composite‑specific Brazier buckling with knockdown factor and modal analysis;
· Halbach coupling with force‑vs‑gap curves and inter‑module crosstalk analysis;
· Actuator stroke‑to‑joint‑angle kinematics with transparent torque‑density scoping;
· Differential photonic sensing with EMI immunity and stiffness‑separated observability;
· Linearised exponential stability with constructive damping coefficient;
· Power budget and scalability demonstration.

To the author’s knowledge, no prior work unifies passive Halbach structural coupling, distributed Lorentz actuation, and differential photonic observability into a single humanoid embodiment framework. The concept of a lightweight, cable‑free, magnetically‑coupled and photonically‑sensed skeletal module is shown to be theoretically well‑posed, linearly exponentially stable, fully observable, and manufacturable with near‑term materials. The explicit scoping of torque‑density limitations positions the PCMSA not as a competitor to high‑torque primary actuators, but as an enabling technology for the dozens of smaller, dexterous degrees of freedom that currently make humanoid robots heavy, complex, and unsafe.

The author acknowledges the independent nature of this research and welcomes collaborative simulation and prototyping partnerships to realise the experimental phases.

