Appendix A: Formal System Definition — Expanded

A.1 Module Domains and Material Properties

Each skeletal module M_i occupies a hollow cylindrical domain

\Omega_i = \left\{ (r,\theta,z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 : R_{\text{inner}} \le r \le R_{\text{outer}},\; 0 \le \theta < 2\pi,\; 0 \le z \le L_i \right\}.

The wall is a symmetric [0^\circ/90^\circ]_s cross‑ply CFRP laminate with fibre volume fraction V_f = 0.6. The homogenised orthotropic stiffness tensor \mathbf{C}^{(i)} in Voigt notation (with the z‑axis as the fibre direction for the 0^\circ plies) has independent constants:

\begin{aligned}
C_{11} &= E_L/(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}),\quad C_{22} = C_{33} = E_T/(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}),\\
C_{12} &= C_{13} = \nu_{LT}E_T/(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL}),\quad C_{23} = \nu_{TT}E_T/(1-\nu_{TT}^2),\\
C_{44} &= G_{LT},\quad C_{55}=C_{66}=G_{TT}.
\end{aligned}

Numerical values are E_L = 135\,\text{GPa}, E_T = 10\,\text{GPa}, \nu_{LT}=0.25, G_{LT}=5\,\text{GPa}, \nu_{TT}=0.35.

A.2 Magnetic Coupling Map \Phi

For the j-th Halbach pair, \Phi_j(\mathbf{x}) is the magnetostatic field computed from the scalar potential \phi_m satisfying \nabla^2\phi_m=0 in the air gap, with boundary conditions \mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{B}=0 on the iron‑free outer surfaces and the Halbach ring magnetisation \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{x}) as a prescribed source. The map \Phi assembles \Phi_j at each structural node.

A.3 Photonic Readout Map \Theta

The mapping from deformation to differential intensity is

\Delta I_k = I_{0} \left[ \exp\!\left(-\int_{\Gamma_k^+} (\alpha_0+\alpha_{\text{bend}}(\kappa(s)))ds\right) - \exp\!\left(-\int_{\Gamma_k^-} (\alpha_0+\alpha_{\text{bend}}(\kappa(s)))ds\right) \right],

where \Gamma_k^\pm are the paths of the two waveguides of the pair. The small‑angle linearisation yields \Delta I_k \approx S_k q_k.

