Appendix B: Structural Mechanics Proofs

B.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (Optimal Hollowness with Composite Brazier Buckling)

Bending stress constraint.
For a cantilever with tip load F_{\text{tip}}, the maximum bending stress occurs at the fixed end:

\sigma_{\max} = \frac{M_{\max} R_{\text{outer}}}{I} = \frac{F_{\text{tip}} L_i \cdot R_{\text{outer}}}{\frac{\pi}{4}(R_{\text{outer}}^4 - R_{\text{inner}}^4)} = \frac{32 F_{\text{tip}} L_i}{\pi D_{\text{outer}}^3 (1-\eta^2)}.

Given a yield stress \sigma_y, we require \sigma_{\max} \le \sigma_y / \text{SF}, leading to

t_{\text{stress}} = R_{\text{outer}} - R_{\text{inner}} \quad \text{such that} \quad \eta = 1 - \frac{2t_{\text{stress}}}{D_{\text{outer}}} \;\text{with the stress equality}.

Composite Brazier buckling constraint.
The classical Brazier moment for an isotropic shell is M_{\text{cr,iso}} = \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{9}\frac{\pi E t^2 R}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu^2)}}. For an orthotropic cylinder, we replace E by the effective hoop modulus E_{\text{hoop}} of the laminate, and introduce a knockdown factor \kappa to account for geometric imperfections. The critical moment becomes

M_{\text{cr}} = \kappa\frac{2\sqrt{2}}{9}\frac{\pi E_{\text{hoop}} t_c^2 R_{\text{outer}}}{\sqrt{3(1-\nu_{LT}\nu_{TL})}}.

Setting M = F_{\text{tip}} L_i \le M_{\text{cr}} yields the minimum wall thickness t_{\text{Brazier}} as given in the text.

The optimal hollowness ratio \eta^* minimises mass while satisfying both constraints. Since mass is proportional to cross‑sectional area \propto 1-\eta, the optimum is achieved at the smallest wall thickness that meets both criteria; hence t_{\min} = \max(t_{\text{stress}}, t_{\text{Brazier}}) and \eta^* = 1 - 2 t_{\min}/D_{\text{outer}}.

Remark on knockdown factor. NASA SP‑8007 is an empirical guideline developed for metallic isotropic cylinders. Its application to composite shells is an active research area; recent NASA studies have employed a universal KDF of 0.65 as a conservative bound for preliminary design. We adopt \kappa=0.65 here with this caveat.

B.2 Proof of Theorem 3.2 (Strut Buckling)

Each internal strut is modelled as a pin‑ended Euler column of length L_{\text{strut}} = 2R_{\text{inner}}/\sin\theta_k. The critical buckling load is P_{\text{cr}} = \pi^2 E_L I_s / L_{\text{strut}}^2, with I_s = \pi r_s^4/4. The required number of struts to support a distributed load P_{\text{distributed}} without buckling is N_{\text{strut}} \ge P_{\text{distributed}}/P_{\text{cr}}. Substituting yields the inequality.

B.3 Modal Analysis Derivation

The first natural frequency of a uniform cantilever beam in bending is \omega_1 = (\beta_1 L_i)^2 \sqrt{E_L I/(\rho_c A L_i^4)} with \beta_1 L_i = 1.875. Substituting the geometry gives the expression in §3.5. For the phalanx module, the computed 480 Hz is well above any expected operational frequency, confirming that dynamic amplification is negligible.

