Appendix C: Halbach Magnetic Coupling Analysis

C.1 Field Enhancement Factor \kappa_H(g)

The magnetic scalar potential for an ideal Halbach ring of infinite axial extent can be expanded in cylindrical harmonics. Retaining the dominant m=1 term for an N_{\text{seg}}-segment ring, the radial component of \mathbf{B} at the median plane z = g/2 is

B_r(r,z) \propto \sin(\pi/N_{\text{seg}}) e^{-r/R_m} \cosh(z/R_m).

Integrating over the pole face and normalising by the simple axial magnetisation case gives the closed‑form \kappa_H(g) stated in the main text. Figure 1 plots \kappa_H(g) for the chosen ring parameters.

C.2 Holding Force Derivation

The Maxwell stress tensor in the air gap is \sigma_{ij}^{\text{mag}} = \frac{1}{\mu_0}(B_i B_j - \frac12 \delta_{ij} B^2). The axial force on a rigid magnet ring is F_z = \oint_S \sigma_{zj}^{\text{mag}} n_j dS over a surface enclosing the ring. Assuming a uniform normal field B_n = B_{\text{gap}} on the pole face area A_{\text{eff}} and zero elsewhere, one obtains F_{\text{hold}} = \frac{B_{\text{gap}}^2 A_{\text{eff}}}{2\mu_0}. The demagnetisation correction C_{\text{gap}}(g) accounts for the reduction in B_{\text{gap}} due to the self‑demagnetising field of the magnets as the gap opens. To first order,

C_{\text{gap}}(g) = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{\mu_r-1}{\mu_r}\frac{g}{h_m}} \approx 1 - \frac{\mu_r-1}{\mu_r}\frac{g}{g+h_m}.

C.3 Inter‑Module Crosstalk

The stray field of a Halbach ring is approximated as a magnetic dipole of moment \mathbf{m} = \frac{B_r V_{\text{mag}}}{\mu_0} \hat{\mathbf{z}}. At a distance d along the axis, B_{\text{stray}} = \frac{\mu_0}{2\pi}\frac{|\mathbf{m}|}{d^3}. For the finger phalanx example, B_{\text{stray}} \approx 0.8\,\text{mT}. The force perturbation on an adjacent ring of identical dimensions is \Delta F \approx \frac{B_{\text{stray}} B_{\text{gap}} A_{\text{eff}}}{\mu_0}, which is less than 2% of the holding force. For smaller spacing, a thin mu‑metal shield (thickness 0.1\,\text{mm}, \mu_r \approx 10^5) placed inside the CFRP wall provides >10× attenuation.

