Appendix D: Actuator Modeling and Joint Kinematics — Complete

D.1 Lorentz Force and Thermal Limit

The coil has N turns, each of length 2w in the field region B_0. The force per unit current is k_F = 2NwB_0. The coil resistance is R_c = \rho_{\text{Cu}} N (2w+2l)/A_{\text{wire}}. At steady state, the temperature rise is \Delta T = I^2 R_c / (h_c A_{\text{surf}}), where h_c is the convection coefficient and A_{\text{surf}} the coil’s surface area. For the example coil, I_{\text{cont}} = 1\,\text{A} gives T_{\text{coil}} < 80^\circ\text{C}.

D.2 Stroke‑to‑Angle Conversion and Multi‑Coil Configuration

A single coil with linear stroke s_{\max} placed at a constant moment arm r yields a joint angle change of \Delta q = s_{\max}/r. To achieve larger ranges, two opposing coils can operate in a push‑pull mode, doubling the effective stroke to 2s_{\max}. A circumferential arrangement of n coils, each acting tangentially on a drum of radius r, could produce continuous rotation if designed as a radial Halbach motor; however, the PCMSA’s current scope focuses on the distributed linear‑stroke approach, which suffices for finger‑like dexterity.

D.3 Active Damping Circuit

The H‑bridge (Fig. 2) allows the coil to be connected either to a current‑source amplifier (for actuation) or to a damping resistor R_d (for passive damping). When damping is active, the back‑EMF V_{\text{emf}} = -k_F \dot{x} drives a current I_d = V_{\text{emf}} / (R_c + R_d), producing a damping force F_d = -k_F I_d = -\frac{k_F^2}{R_c+R_d} \dot{x}. Thus c_{\text{em}} = k_F^2 / (R_c+R_d). A simple logic circuit senses the zero‑crossing of the commanded current and switches the bridge state within microseconds.

