Appendix E: Photonic Sensing and Observability — Proofs

E.1 Bending Loss Model

For a multimode step‑index waveguide, the power attenuation coefficient due to bending is empirically well described by \alpha_{\text{bend}} = C_1 \exp(-C_2/\kappa), where \kappa is the local curvature. In the linear regime (\kappa \ll C_2), we may expand \alpha_{\text{bend}} \approx \alpha_0 + \eta \kappa, but we retain the full form for accuracy. The transmitted intensity is given by the line integral along the waveguide.

E.2 Stiffness‑Separation Condition and Diagonal Jacobian

Let q_j be the joint angle between modules j and j+1. The curvature experienced by a waveguide passing through the joint is \kappa(s) \approx q_j / L_{\text{joint}}, where L_{\text{joint}} is the effective bending length at the coupling. Between joints, the waveguide is bonded to the module surface and experiences curvature due to module bending. The module bending curvature under joint loading is \kappa_m = M/(E_L I) \approx (F_{\text{tip}} L_i)/(E_L I). The condition |\kappa_m| \ll |\kappa_{\text{joint}}| for all joints leads to the stiffness‑separation ratio R_{\text{stiff}}. With R_{\text{stiff}} \gg 20, the cross‑talk between joints is negligible, and the Jacobian becomes diagonal with entries S_j = \partial (\Delta I_j)/\partial q_j.

E.3 Proof of Theorem 8.1 (Observability)

Given the diagonal Jacobian, the observability Gramian \mathbf{W}_o = \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}^T \mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}} is a diagonal matrix with entries S_j^2 > 0. Since all diagonal entries are strictly positive, \operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{W}_o) = N_{\text{DOF}}. Thus the linearised system is observable. The condition number \kappa(\mathbf{J}_{\text{photo}}) = \max_j S_j / \min_j S_j remains bounded, ensuring robust numerical inversion.

E.4 EMI Immunity Proof (Theorem 6.1)

The Faraday rotation angle in an optical fibre is \theta_F = V_F \int \mathbf{B}_{\text{EMI}} \cdot d\mathbf{l}, with V_F \approx 1.5\,\text{rad T}^{-1}\text{m}^{-1} for PDMS. The stray magnetic field at the waveguide location from a coil is at most B_{\text{EMI}} \sim 1\,\text{mT} over a length \sim 0.2\,\text{m}, giving \theta_F \sim 3 \times 10^{-4}\,\text{rad}. Intensity‑based sensing is insensitive to polarisation rotation to this degree; the induced intensity change is \Delta I/I \approx \theta_F^2 < 10^{-6}. The readout is therefore immune.

